//go:build protocoldef

package protocoldef

// BattleFramePush declares the message ID and generated schema artifact of
// one lockstep broadcast packet: the frames the room has cut, most recent
// last, encoded by roost-core/sync/lockstep. The room sends the packet bytes
// themselves through lockstep.TCPSender (RS v2 Lockstep flag), without
// serializing this PB wrapper — the same arrangement as EntitySyncPush. The
// packet holds the last few frames rather than only the newest one, so a
// client that missed a push heals from the next one without asking for
// anything — that redundancy is why lockstep tolerates a lossy lane. A UDP or
// KCP deployment sends the same bytes as a datagram.
//
// The packet carries no battle id: a connection receives the frames of the
// rooms it has spoken in, and the demo's client plays one battle at a time.
//
// A client decodes it with lockstep.FrameAssembler (roost-core/robot's
// LockstepBot does exactly that) and applies the frames in order.
type BattleFramePush struct {
	Packet []byte `pb:"1"`
}

//roost:protocol group=game handler=player
type BattleFrameProtocol interface {
	//roost:msg id=10102
	BattleFrame() BattleFramePush
}
